// DMA+PIO schedule for example 22-dma-pio-demo — the load-bearing
// schedule for this example, and the whole point of the demo.
//
// Two workers (same topology as 02-split-add):
//   host  -- runs the effectful I/O kernels (load_samples, load_gains,
//            save_output). Owns the file system / the injected input
//            region under Renode.
//   w0    -- runs the pure `apply_gain` kernel; the loop body executes
//            here.
//
// Cross-worker dataflow edges (host <-> w0), one `transfer` per
// crossing data symbol (PRD §6.3.4: omitting a required transfer is a
// hard error, not a silent default). What makes THIS schedule the demo
// is the per-edge `mode=` hint (TASK-0438.01 DSL, .02 codegen):
//
//   samples (host -> w0)  mode=dma  -- BULK audio frame in. Bulk frame
//                                      transfers are the natural DMA
//                                      case: arm a descriptor, let the
//                                      stream run. Emits dma_link_arm
//                                      (push side) + dma_link_recv_arm
//                                      (recv side) + a completion spin.
//   gains   (host -> w0)  mode=pio  -- per-sample gain COEFFICIENTS.
//                                      Control/coefficient words are the
//                                      natural PIO case: a handful of
//                                      CPU-driven loads, no descriptor
//                                      setup overhead. Emits the
//                                      unchanged link_push / link_recv.
//   out     (w0 -> host)  mode=dma  -- processed BULK frame back. DMA
//                                      again (bulk), symmetric to
//                                      `samples`.
//
// So this single schedule emits BOTH transport shapes: two DMA-async
// edges (samples, out) and one PIO-sync edge (gains). That is parent
// TASK-0438 AC#1 (>=1 dma edge AND >=1 pio edge in one app).
//
// HONEST timing-model note (parent AC#4)
// --------------------------------------
// `mode=dma` here is a MODELLED DMA SHAPE, not a silicon STM32H7 DMA
// stream. Under Renode the default `dma_link_arm` delegates to the same
// `link_push` UART transport and `dma_link_poll` returns `true`
// synchronously, so the emitted spin loop terminates immediately and
// the DMA bytes ride the SAME UART fabric as the PIO bytes. The two
// modes are therefore VALUE-EQUIVALENT and the whole app stays
// byte-exact under `just renode-multimcu 22-dma-pio-demo dma_pio`. The
// proof is value-correctness, NOT timing. A real async DMA engine is
// the follow-up TASK-0048.12.
//
// All three transfers stay `sync` (producer blocks until the consumer
// has received). `mode=` is ORTHOGONAL to sync/async: it selects the
// transport CODE PATH, not the completion semantics. Keeping them
// `sync` is what lets the synchronous embedded shim honour them without
// a capability lie (same surface the embedded-pattern capabilities.toml
// declares: default class, heap region, sync, no async/buffer/event).
//
// What's intentionally NOT here
// -----------------------------
//   - No loop options (no block=, no vectorize=, no pipeline=).
//   - No notify=, no buffer=N. `sync` + `mode=` only.
//   - No distributed placement. A single `w0` runs the whole loop.

schedule for "../prog.algo.nuc" {
    workers = { host, w0 };

    // I/O on host (owns the file system / injected input region).
    place load_samples on host;
    place load_gains   on host;
    place save_output  on host;

    // Compute on w0.
    place apply_gain   on w0;

    // Cross-worker data — one transfer per data symbol that crosses,
    // each tagged with its transport mode (the demo's payload).
    transfer samples : sync, mode=dma;   // bulk audio frame  -> DMA-async
    transfer gains   : sync, mode=pio;   // gain coefficients -> PIO-sync
    transfer out     : sync, mode=dma;   // processed frame   -> DMA-async
}
